chuckles back now with part two of  series is college worth it the key to  success in this country and a degree  from a brand name school as a virtual  guarantee of future wealth and power and  we believe it is then one of the most  important questions we can ask about  America is this how exactly did you get  into a prestigious university what are  the standards  well remarkably nobody really knows the  answer  college admissions is a bewildering  mystery to most of us it's a treasure  map with the directions obscured and  that's not an accident colleges  consistently refused to explain how they  admit students usually it takes legal  action to find out secrecy is the key to  their power and they guard it jealously  but there are some facts available  thanks to whistleblowers in college  admissions offices in a series of civil  rights lawsuits filed over the years we  do know something about admission  standards the main thing to understand  going in is that no matter what you may  have heard higher education is not  really a meritocracy there aren't a lot  of slow kids at Harvard but that doesn't  mean the smartest and hardest-working  applicants get in often they don't get  in other factors come into play  the first and probably the least  publicized of these is legacy admissions  you're more likely to get into a  prestigious school if your parents went  there much more likely it turns out a  2011 study of 30 elite universities  found that the children of undergraduate  alumni were on average 45 percent more  likely to get in an earlier study found  the legacy advantage was equivalent to  an additional hundred and sixty points  on the SAT that's a major leg up  colleges understand that admitting the  kids of alumni help with fundraising  they don't like to talk about that but  it's true the other factor that colleges  don't like to talk about is affirmative  action the Supreme Court banned the use  of racial quotas in admissions some  years ago but in real life that decision  meant nothing skin color remains a  central consideration at every level of  the process  the average admissions offices every bit  as race-conscious as any institution in  the Jim Crow South and  are less transparent about it the  numbers tell the story every study there  have been a few of them has shown a huge  discrepancy between the achievement that  colleges require of applicants of  different racial groups this is true at  the undergraduate level and at the  graduate level the new analysis of  recent data from the Association of  American Medical Colleges for example  found that African American students  with low scores in the med school  admissions test an average grades had a  fifty-six percent chance of getting into  med school at the other end  Asian students with the exact same  scores and grades had a six percent  chance of getting in so facts like these  have spurred lawsuits from groups  individuals alleging discrimination and  in every case the data they've unearthed  proof there's been discrimination  defenders of the current system though  say that nobody is being discriminated  against and that race has nothing to do  with these numbers of course no one  actually believes that consider this  example at Cal Tech which has a rare  race-neutral admissions policy the  student body was 43% Asian in 2013 at  Harvard which erect quotas against  Asians as it once did against Jews that  number was 18% roughly where it has been  for years even as the number of Asian  applicants has risen when skin color  becomes a criterion for admission some  people lose but who exactly wins no one  ever asked that now we know who's  supposed to benefit affirmative action  was designed to level the playing field  for Americans whose ancestors had been  discriminated against primarily this  meant black applicants yet fifty years  after affirmative action began the  percentage of African Americans that  many elite colleges appears to be  dropping even as schools boast about  being diverse how is that happening  simple foreigners are taking their place  under the rules of race-conscious  admissions the son of a Nigerian tycoon  meets the same standards and checks the  same box as the son of a single mother  from the southside of Chicago  both are black so for the purposes of  diversity goals they're exactly the same  but in fact they're not the same at all  one is poor American and descended from  people brought to this country by force  you probably want to help that kid the  other is a rich foreigner well-connected  and far more likely to have attended as  elective private school he doesn't need  your help yet who has a better chance of  leveraging diversity standards to get  into Harvard you know the answer the  rich kid does he gets an advantage he  hasn't earned and doesn't deserve and  that's a win for him  Harvard wins too it gets to brag about  being inclusive meanwhile the kid on the  south side of Chicago doesn't go to  college at all this is happening at  schools all over this country and the  trend is accelerating as immigration  accelerates the 2007 study found that  among black students at the top 28  American universities the percentage of  immigrants was about double the  representation in the broader population  at Ivy League school students from  immigrant families accounted for fully  41 percent of black freshmen at Harvard  a study three years earlier found that  two-thirds of all black undergraduates  were from immigrant backgrounds this is  the opposite of what affirmative action  was designed to do yet over time the  definition of who was eligible for a  front of action has expanded even  further moving even farther away from  where it began in many places illegal  aliens now qualify for lower admission  standards universally identifying as  Hispanic rather than white gives  applicants the equivalent of a hundred  and thirty points on the SAT so this is  a huge advantage for newly arrived  immigrants over native-born Americans  yet it's puzzling if you think about it  why should the descendant of Spanish  conquistadors get preference over the  great grandchild of indentured Irish  servants what injustice is resolved by  putting one over the other how is that  fair how is it just well of course it  isn't fair or just which is why it has  never explained or defended only imposed  Americans lose in this arrangement  African Americans get shafted completely  in favor of foreigners with no  connection to this country or its  history yet few complain about this  because almost nobody really understands  what is happening and that's why the  admissions process is secret so you  won't challenge it Kathryn layman is  chairman of the u.s. Commission on civil  rights she spoke with us about all of  this just a minute ago  thank you very much for coming on thank  you for having so what's the purpose of  affirmative action what do we have it  well the goal is to make sure that our  colleges and universities have a chance  to achieve the diversity that they say  that they want for their schools where  the long-standing precedent is that they  can use affirmative action if there are  no other means to achieve that diversity  and to be able to satisfy their  education on this so it's not a an  attempt to redress past wrongs it's  simply for the sake of variety well it  started as an attempt to address past  wrongs I think that goal continues to  exist but the reality is that schools  have of long-standing said that what  they want to do is to educate students  to live in work in be prepared for an  increasingly global society and to be  able to be full participants in the  world that they will enter and so  achieving diversity is one way of making  sure that students are able to be  educated toward that end so if you  factor sports out of it and this has  been done and you reduce it to grades  and test scores it still remains true  that Asians East Asians Chinese  particularly in Koreans score what five  hundred points higher than other groups  with the same admissions rates and so  here's a here's a flaw in your analysis  yes you're reducing it to the test  scores and the grades which the court  has said specifically you cannot do the  the school's the colleges the  universities have a First Amendment  academic freedom interest in selecting  whom they want to educate and how they  want to educate them and what they say  discrimination and if we're happening to  any other group of course there'd be a  total outrage well I think I think it  would be a total outrage for  discrimination period what the court has  said is that that's not how to analyze  it and that that's not discrimination  the court has said you take a look at  whether the school is analyzing all of  who a child is no I'm aware what the  court says I just don't think it passes  the common sense test but do you think  if you can see that part of the purpose  of affirmative action and I think the  purpose that most Americans the debt  would identify with if there's any part  of it that they would is the idea that  you know there are groups in American  particularly African Americans who are  discriminated against sis directly and  this is a way of making good on that  birth trying so how does it make you  feel when you see the numbers  that foreign-born black students  displace african-american students  especially prestigious schools at a  really high rate and then the school  gets to say we're diverse when they're  letting in you know the kid of the  Nigerian ambassador doesn't that seem a  perversion of the purpose well I think  it might be a perversion of the purpose  I haven't seen those numbers and that's  not my experience from investigating the  schools and in fact what we're seeing  now in particular in this administration  with its hostility to having people come  to this country from other countries  we're seeing a drop-off in international  students at our schools in a way that is  affecting the bottom line but let's say  something let's say I'll get the exact  numbers in a sec but let's say you know  I don't know 19% of all black people in  the United States were foreign-born but  the percentage of black students at  prestigious schools were foreign-born  was like 45% that would suggest that  they're disproportionately getting the  slots that most of us think are going to  African Americans you don't think that's  a problem  well I again we need to resist your  reduction of the applicant to a  particular characteristic rather than  look at the whole police force of course  reduce them to that I mean they're  really very conscious in the way that  they do admission you know let me tell  you that that is just not true that the  schools are pretty opaque in the way  that they do their admissions and that's  that's a problem for the American public  but I've overseen investigations in  college and universities unexecuted is  topic and what I've seen time and time  and time again is that the schools  actually do a very good job of taking a  look at what the students say about  themselves what their recommenders time  is why can't we judge it for ourselves  since this is the gateway to wealth and  power in this country a degree from this  prestigious school and the government  subsidies are overwhelming and the tax  breaks are overwhelming why don't I have  a right to know what the criteria are  that they're using you know Tucker I'm a  huge believer in transparency I think it  would be better for the American public  to have more information when I was at  the office for civil rights we entered  into a resolution agreement with  Princeton on this topic and we evaluated  exactly this question and we made our  analysis very public to be able to share  what people's questions were and what  our analysis was one totally with you on  that I think knowing more is better  Kevin thanks for joining us thanks very  much for having me
 

